## 1. Architecture at a Glance

```
                ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
                │              Splunk (prod logs)                  │
                │  read-only service account + Splunk MCP server   │
                └───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┘
                                │ SPL queries via MCP
        ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
        │  Agent 1: sw-splunk-triage                     │
        │  find, dedupe & classify error signatures      │
        └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                │ Defect Dossier (signature, stack
                                │ trace, volume, first-seen, users hit)
        ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
        │  Agent 2: sw-root-cause                        │
        │  stack trace → code path in monolith,          │
        │  git-history correlation, RCA hypothesis       │
        └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                │ RCA Report (file:line, commit,
                                │ hypothesis, confidence, blast radius)
        ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
        │  Agent 3: sw-fix-engineer                      │
        │  minimal fix + unit test + validation          │
        │  ── human review gate ──                       │
        └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                │
                     Draft PR / Jira ticket + RCA doc
```

Three agents rather than one keeps each context window focused (Splunk-speak vs. codebase-speak vs. fix-writing), and each hand-off produces a reviewable artifact — which is exactly where your human-in-the-loop gates sit.

